Now I have a decent picture. Let me design.

Key design decisions:

### 1. Stale judgement: one spelling in domain

A new module `src/domain/work/stale-lock.ts` (or extend `run-report.ts`). The signal: a node's thread has an open claim naming a run id; that run has finished (run facts from Actions API: status completed); and there is no ending answering that claim after the claim. Actually the acceptance:

- "run が終了済みで未 release の claim" → sweep releases
- fresh claim (run 実行中) → sweep does nothing
- park: waiting ending whose run finished and no resume → return to person

So the domain function should be something like:

```ts
export interface LockFacts {
  /** the open claim the shaped thread leaves, ask + run id */
  claim?: { ask: number; run: number };
  /** whether the run that claimed is known to have finished */
  runFinished: boolean;
  /** whether the claim's ask already has an ending (release present) */
  released: boolean;
  /** fallback: claim age in ms when the run id can't be resolved */
  ageMs?: number;
}
export function staleLock(facts): boolean
```

Hmm. Simpler: a single function `lockIsStale` in `domain/work/thread.ts`? The issue says "domain 側に共通関数を置き、sweep と guard の両方が使う。信号は run-facts 主(Run 完了 + release 無し)、タイマーは補助(claim が run id を名乗れない場合の fallback 定数)".

So: `domain/work/stale-lock.ts` exporting:

```ts
/** How long an unresolvable claim has to be old before it reads as dead. */
export const STALE_LOCK_FALLBACK_MS = ...;

export type LeadRunFact = "finished" | "running" | "unknown";

export function lockIsStale(input: { run: ... , claimedAt?: string, now }): boolean
```

Let me think about how the guard actually gets the facts. The guard currently calls `commentWouldBeRemoved(repo, number, commentId)` which returns `{removed, parked}`. To deliver the run id to the guard, I need `commentWouldBeRemoved` (or a sibling) to also return the open claim's run. `shapedThread` already computes openClaim {ask, run}; `commentWouldBeRemoved` reads `shapedThread(comments.map(entryOf), readers)` — but only uses `shaped.events`. So the run id is right there, available. Wait, the issue says "shapedThread が parse して捨てている claim の run id" — actually shapedThread keeps it in `openClaim`. Hmm, the issue text says `src/domain/work/thread.ts:474-489` (`shapedThread`) drops it, and guard doesn't get it. Actually it's kept. But `commentWouldBeRemoved` returns only removed/parked. So I need to add the claim to `WouldBeRemoved`.

Wait, actually there's a subtlety: `commentWouldBeRemoved` for the "listed" case uses `keptCommentIds(comments).has(listed.id)` — a second shape. But for parked it uses `shaped.events`. I can extend the `WouldBeRemoved` interface with `claim?: {ask, run}` and also the claim's timestamp? Hmm, for the fallback timer we need the claim's age. The `at` of the claim entry is available in `readComments` entries (DatedEntry has `at`). But `shapedThread` doesn't carry the claim's entry. Hmm.

Simplify: fallback timer. When the claim's run id cannot be resolved to a run fact (e.g. Actions API can't be read, or run=0), fall back to a timer constant: claim comment older than N (say 60 minutes? run job timeout is 240 min... hmm). Actually the job timeout is 240 minutes (JOB_TIMEOUT_MINUTES = 240). So a run is bounded by 4 hours. A fallback constant should be > that, e.g. 6 hours? Hmm, that's long. But a fallback that fires early would delete a person's... no wait, stale → do NOT delete. So the fallback firing early means keeping the comment (safe direction). So the fallback can be generous-but-not-infinite: e.g., 12 hours? Or should it be conservative in the other direction? Stale means "don't delete the human comment", so a too-eager stale verdict = guard stops guarding = a human comment gets through to a running agent. The cost of that is small (the comment just sits there / races). The cost of too-late is the deadlock observed. Since job timeout is 240 min, a run cannot live beyond 4h. So fallback = 6 hours is a sound upper bound on a live run: past 4h the runner job was killed by its own timeout, so its claim cannot be live... well, a queued run could wait? No, a queued run has no claim (claim is written at start).

Actually wait: the claim is written by the runner at start, and the runner job's timeout is 240 min. But the job is not just the runner step — JOB_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=240 is the whole job. So after 4h the job is force-cancelled and the run completes. So a claim older than ~4h05m cannot belong to a live run... unless GitHub is slow to start? No: claim is at start of job.

Hmm but careful: a run can be re-run (rerun of a workflow) — same run id, new attempt, new claim? Claim_turn notes "a line of the same run id is an EARLIER ATTEMPT of this run (a re-run keeps github.run_id)". So a re-run of an old run could re-use the claim? The claim line stays; the re-run posts a new claim? It says "A claim already on the marker is never taken back" — so a re-run writing a claim for the same ask with the same run id is the same line. Fine.

I'll pick the fallback constant = 6 hours with the reason: the runner job's own `timeout-minutes` is 240, so past six hours no run that wrote the claim can still be running.

Now, where does the run-fact read live? Actions API: `gh api repos/{repo}/actions/runs/{run_id} --jq '.status'` → "completed" or not. That's an adapter: `src/adapters/github/run-facts.ts` with `readRunState(repo, runId): "finished" | "running" | "unknown"`. Hmm — but the issue mentions "actions/runs の既存 reader は `src/adapters/github/merge-signals.ts:208` 等" — which lists runs by head_sha. I'll add a small reader in a new adapter file `run-status.ts`, or add to an existing adapter. Let me create `src/adapters/github/run-status.ts`:

```ts
/** Whether the run that claimed a turn is still going, finished, or cannot be told. */
export type RunLife = "finished" | "running" | "unknown";
export function readRunLife(repo: string, runId: number): RunLife
```

Implementation: `ghRead("api", `repos/${repo}/actions/runs/${runId}`, "--jq", ".status")`; code !== 0 → "unknown"; stdout "completed" → finished; anything else → running. Note merge-signals comments: "Naming the one value that is finished is what keeps a status this file has not seen from reading as 'there is nothing running here'" — same convention here: only "completed" is finished.

### 2. The domain rule: one spelling

`src/domain/work/stale-lock.ts`:

```ts
export type LockRunLife = "finished" | "running" | "unknown";

/** Fallback: how old an unresolvable claim must be to read as dead. 6h > runner job timeout 240min */
export const STALE_CLAIM_FALLBACK_MS = 6 * 60 * 60 * 1000;

export interface LockState {
  /** Life of the run the claim names; "unknown" when it names none or could not be read. */
  run: LockRunLife;
  /** Whether an ending on the thread already answered this claim (released). */
  released: boolean;
  /** Age of the claim comment, in ms; undefined when its time could not be read. */
  ageMs?: number;
}

/** Whether a lock whose claim is on a finished run and unreleased is dead. */
export function lockIsStale(state: LockState): boolean
```

Logic: if released → false (fresh/released → do not intervene). If run === "finished" → true. If run === "running" → false. If unknown → ageMs !== undefined && ageMs >= STALE_CLAIM_FALLBACK_MS.

Wait, "release 無し" — with the claim model, `shapedThread` keeps the claim open only while it is unanswered; if an ending answers it, openClaim is undefined. So "open claim" already implies unreleased. Hmm, except the `waiting` park case: the waiting ending answers the ask but the claim stays open (park). So for park, "released" needs a different read: a later `done`/`stopped` ending answering the ask, or a new run claiming the parked ask.

Let me think about how the sweep reads a node:

For each candidate node:
1. Read the thread once (`readRunWatchThread` gives dated, entries, events). Hmm, but I need shaped comments with ids and times and the openClaim. Let me add an adapter function that returns everything needed:

```ts
export interface OpenLock {
  claim: { ask: number; run: number };
  /** When the claim comment was written ("" when unreadable). */
  claimedAt: string;
  /** The newest turn event is `waiting` — a park. */
  parked: boolean;
  /** ... */
}
export function openLockOn(repo, number): OpenLock | undefined
```

Actually `openClaimOn` in thread.ts already gives `{ask, run, parked, claimed}`. I need `claimedAt` too. Let me extend `openClaimOn` to include `at` (claim comment's created_at). It reads `shaped.comments` entries which are DatedEntry with `at`. But `openClaimOn` reads `shaped.openClaim` — the shaping doesn't tell me which entry held the claim. But the last claim... Hmm. `shaped.comments[last]` is the newest kept comment; for `claimed` it checks `readers.claim(last.body) !== undefined`. For the parked-not-reclaimed case, the last comment is the waiting ending, not the claim.

Simplest robust approach: find the newest non-quoted comment in the kept list that carries a claim with ask === openClaim.ask; its `at` is the claim time. Hmm, but if a re-invoked run appended the took line to the same marker... the marker's at would be older than the new attempt. Edge case; acceptable — fallback only, and it errs toward stale (older) which is the safe direction for the guard? No wait — erring toward stale means NOT deleting the human comment. For the sweep, erring toward stale means releasing. Hmm, for a truly-live run whose claim comment is old (a long-running run), releasing would be wrong-ish: it would post a missing-ending notice while the run is still working. That's a false notice. But the run-fact signal is primary, so the timer only fires when we can't read the run. Acceptable and documented.

Actually for the sweep, maybe simpler: the sweep iterates nodes and uses run facts; the timer only applies when run id is 0 or unreadable. Since run=0 means no run id (shouldn't happen normally since TOOK_TAG requires a run), and unreadable = API failure. On API failure, maybe better to skip entirely (no-op, retry next sweep) than to use a timer? The issue says the timer is for "claim が run id を名乗れない場合の fallback". readRunLife returning "unknown" includes both "could not read" and "no id". If the API read fails, acting on a timer could be wrong in either direction. Hmm, but it's the same function for guard and sweep and the issue asks for one spelling. I'll make it: unknown + old enough → stale. Since the guard's stale verdict only means "keep the comment", the downside there is minimal. For the sweep, the downside is a false notice on a live run. To keep the sweep safe, the sweep can require a *confirmed* finished run (`run === "finished"`) — no, that breaks "one spelling".

Alternative: the sweep uses the fallback but the fallback is generous (6h > 4h job timeout), so a false notice requires the API read to fail for a run older than 6h — impossible for a live run. So both are sound with the 6h constant. Good: one spelling, both callers, no divergence.

### 3. Sweep implementation

Where? The issue says reconcile gets a sweep mode: `--sweep`. It's a separate script or a flag on reconcile_run_ending? The issue says "reconcile に sweep モード(例: `--sweep`)を渡す引数の組み立てを wac 側で行う". So add `--sweep` to `reconcile_run_ending.ts`. But the file is already ~300 lines with a big header; adding a sweep makes it big. Cleaner: put the sweep logic in a lib module `src/entrypoints/machinery/lib/sweep-locks.ts` (app-level, testable through the CLI) and have `reconcile_run_ending.ts` dispatch to it when `--sweep` is passed. Hmm, but `lib/` modules in machinery are pure-ish helpers (turn-labels.ts does gh calls, so gh calls in lib are fine).

Structure:
- `src/domain/work/stale-lock.ts` — the rule + constant.
- `src/adapters/github/run-status.ts` — run life read.
- `src/adapters/github/thread.ts` — extend `openClaimOn` to carry the claim's own `at`, and `commentWouldBeRemoved` to carry the claim (`{ask, run, at}`).
- `src/entrypoints/machinery/lib/sweep-stale-locks.ts` — enumerate candidates, judge, release.
- `src/entrypoints/machinery/reconcile_run_ending.ts` — `--sweep` flag → run the sweep, else the existing path.
- `src/entrypoints/machinery/guard_comment_during_run.ts` — read the claim, ask run facts, if stale: do not delete; post a notice? The issue: "削除の代わりに、既存の引用通知の仕組みに合わせて「lock が stale である」ことを示す通知を出すか、単に削らない(どちらかを既存テストの文言体系に合わせて選ぶ)". Simpler and less risky: simply do not delete (leave the comment alone), and log. But then the person's comment stays and won't be acted on either (blocked=false means it will be dispatched/parsed → a new run gets dispatched which may collide with... well, the run is dead, so a new run is what we want). Hmm: if the guard doesn't block, the comment proceeds: parse → dispatch a runner. That's good: the person's comment starts a run, which is the recovery. But the stale claim is still open on the thread; the new run's claim will be a new claim (openClaim replaced). And the sweep will also release, posting a notice. Two mechanisms racing? Both are idempotent-ish.

But careful: if guard doesn't block, the comment gets dispatched and a new run starts, then the sweep runs and sees... the old claim? No — the new claim replaced openClaim (newest claim wins). The new run's claim names a live run → not stale. So fine. But if the sweep runs between the guard and the new run's claim, the sweep releases (posts notice + label moves to waiting-for-person) while a dispatch is in flight. The label would then be overwritten by the new run's `running` phase. OK, harmless.

Alternatively: the guard keeps the comment (not deleted) but sets blocked=true? No — then the comment never reaches anyone and the person is stuck again. Better: don't delete → let it through. Hmm, but blocked=false is what the workflow uses to NOT dispatch... let me check: `if: blocked != 'true'` gates the dispatch. So not-deleted + blocked=false → dispatch happens. Good. But that means during a genuinely stale lock, a person's comment starts a new run — exactly the recovery path.

Hmm wait, one risk: guard "stale" judgement requires a run-facts API call per comment on a node with an open claim. That's fine (only when a claim is open).

Also: should the guard post a notice saying the lock is stale? The issue allows either. Simpler: no notice (just don't delete). But a person would then get no explanation at all — which is fine, because their comment is acted on (a run starts). Actually currently when the guard doesn't block, the machinery proceeds normally: comment remains, dispatch happens. So silence is right. I'll pick "do not delete" and log to stderr. Document why.

Hmm, but what if the comment is a `/stop`? That's handled elsewhere.

What about the `several` commands rule — that's independent and remains.

Now the sweep's release action. Existing reconcile's capabilities: post the missing-ending notice (`missingEndingNotice` with `answers=ask run=<watchRunId>`) + `applyTurnLabels("with-person")`. For the sweep, the "watch run id" is not this sweep's run; we should use the dead run's id (sweep knows it from the claim). That's exactly right: the notice's `run=<dead run>` is the identity of the run being reported. And `answers=<ask>` closes the ask. Then the next sweep finds no open claim → no-op (idempotent). 

For parks: the thread's open claim is the parked ask, the run that parked has finished (its waiting ending names it), and no resume came. Release: post an ending answering the parked ask, verb `done`? Hmm. Careful: `parkExit` writes verb `stopped` answers=ask run=0 for a person's stop. For the sweep, the correct verb... The node was parked waiting for children; if the children are all done but the parent never resumed, the parent should be re-invoked — but that's aggregation's job, not the sweep's. The issue says: "park も同様: 待機 ending の run が済んでいて resume が来ていない node を人に返す". So return to the person: an ending with verb `done` (gives the node back) answering the parked ask. Whose run id? The sweep's own run id would be a lie; the parked run's id is truthful ("the run that parked this ended"). Hmm — the ending record's `run` field means "which run wrote this ending" per the tag docs ("run is the run id of the taker"). For the sweep's notice, `run=<watchRunId>` in the existing code is described as "this watch's id". Hmm: `endingIdentity` uses GITHUB_RUN_ID. In `reconcile_run_ending`, `missingEndingNotice(..., {ask, watchRunId})` → `run: watchRunId`, and its comment says "it is an ending carrying `answers=<ask> run=<this watch's id>`". So `run` = the writing run. For the sweep, the writing run is the sweep's own run id — available as `GITHUB_RUN_ID`. Using the dead run's id would be a lie in that field and would make `endingLeftBy(deadRun)` true — which is actually harmless/beneficial? Hmm, `endingLeftBy` is used by the per-run watch path only, keyed on the triggered run id; a sweep writing `run=<dead run>` would make the watch's "did this run leave its ending" true for the dead run — meaning if the watch fires later for that dead run, it stays silent. That's good (idempotence). But is it a lie? The field means "the run this ending reports about / the run that wrote it" — ambiguous. `claim`'s `run` = the taker. `ending`'s `run` = the writer per turnHeader docs ("the id of the run that wrote it"). Hmm, "The ask comment id this ending answers, and the id of the run that wrote it (#285)". So run = writer. So the sweep should write its own GITHUB_RUN_ID. Then idempotence comes from `answers=<ask>` making the claim closed (shapedThread closes the claim when an ending answers it and the event is not `waiting`). 

So the sweep writes verb `done`, answers=<ask>, run=<sweep run id> — for a dead-run claim that's the same shape as the existing missing-ending notice. And that's exactly what makes "以降の sweep がそれ以上書き込まない (idempotent)" true: next sweep sees no open claim.

Hold on: the existing notice says "this request was taken up, but no answer was posted for it". For park (children done, parent never resumed), that text is wrong-ish. Different text: a park whose children finished but no resume arrived is a different failure. Let me have two notices? The issue's acceptance for park is just "人に返す" tests. I'll write one notice function that takes a verb and a reason line, or two small functions. Let's keep it minimal:

- Dead claim (no ending): existing `missingEndingNotice` — reuse it verbatim (same meaning: the run claimed and never answered).
- Park: `parkedLockReturnedNotice` — "the run that parked this node on the work under it finished, and nothing resumed it. The turn is yours again." with verb `done`.

Hmm, is releasing a park automatically correct? What if the children are still running? Then the parent parked legitimately and the aggregation will resume it — the sweep would steal the turn and hand it to a person, and the aggregation's resume would... The issue says "待機 ending の run が済んでいて resume が来ていない node を人に返す". A parked parent waiting on children: children still running for hours is normal. So a sweep that hands the parent back to the person whenever the parked run finished would be wrong for the most common case! We must check the children: park is stale only if the children are all finished AND no resume came. That's aggregation's question (`countOpenSiblings`). Hmm, that's a heavier read (GraphQL per node) and the sweep would be doing aggregation's job.

Hmm. Let me reconsider: what distinguishes the #12 incident? The park was left with `atomaton:ended=waiting answers=...` and the claim remained; the parent should have been re-invoked when the children finished, but the re-invocation's run died. Wait no — in #12 the run died at context limit; the park was stale because... The 441 comment says the state was "claim + park both left, no user escape". The parked parent's children — for #12's case? Actually #12 was a sub-issue... The guard deleted `/atomaton recover` because the thread said agent-held.

Given the risk of stealing a legitimate park, I'll include the sibling check: a park is stale only when the node has no open launched sub-issue left (i.e. aggregation should have resumed it) AND a grace period since the parked run finished... Hmm, "resume が来ていない" is the signal. A parked node with no open children and no resume means the aggregation failed → hand to person. That's precise and cheap enough via `countOpenSiblings` (one GraphQL + one comment read per open child — could be costly in a sweep across all in-progress nodes). Hmm, but parks are rarer than running claims.

Alternatively use `issueLinks` (one GraphQL) and check open children with the sub-issue label + launched (needs per-child thread read). Hmm.

Let me reconsider the scope: this is one leaf issue and it's already large (schedule + sweep + guard + domain rule + tests). The park half needs care. Options:

(a) Park: release when the parked run finished AND no open sub-issue remains (using `countOpenSiblings`), which is exactly the aggregation's premiscondition — if that returns 0, the parent should have been re-invoked; nobody came; hand to person. Use it.

(b) Simpler: park: release when the parked run finished and the thread shows no claim newer than the waiting ending, and there are no open children.

I'll do (a) with `countOpenSiblings`, and catch its throw as "don't touch" (a failed read isn't an answer).

Hmm, wait. Does releasing a park duplicate the aggregation dispatch? If all children are done, `dispatchOrchestratorIfReady` would dispatch the parent — but it only runs on a child's close event or a PR merge, which already happened. If it failed then, nothing retries. The sweep handing the turn to a person is the safety net. But if a resume dispatch IS in flight (dispatched, run not started yet, claim not written), the sweep might race it: children all closed, aggregation dispatched a resume run, that run hasn't claimed yet, so thread still shows park + old claim stale → sweep hands to person, then the resume run claims and works. Result: an extra "handed back" comment while a run works; the run's own ending follows. Minor noise, and the shape is the same as the existing watch's insurance. Acceptable but I should reduce it: require the parked run to have finished *and* no newer comment on the thread at all? A resume dispatch writes a dispatch marker comment (in-progress + marker) before dispatching — `postDispatchMarker`? Let me check: dispatchRunner posts a marker comment when it dispatches (that's the ask). So a resume in flight has a marker comment newer than the waiting ending → the thread's newest comment is the marker, which means the sweep can see "something arrived after the park". So: park is stale only when the *newest* comment on the thread is the claim or the waiting ending — i.e. nothing arrived after it that could be a resume. `shapedThread` gives `openClaim` and events; `openClaimOn` gives `claimed` = newest comment is itself a claim. Hmm.

Let me define the park release condition concretely in the sweep:
- shaped thread's newest event is `waiting` (parked=true)
- the claim is open, its run is finished (or fallback timer)
- the newest comment is not a claim (`claimed=false` — a re-invoked run has claimed the parked ask, so a run is live)
- no open launched children remain (countOpenSiblings === 0)

That covers "resume が来ていない" reasonably: a resume that started would have claimed.

OK. And each of these plus the guard share `lockIsStale` for the run-life part. But the park needs the extra conditions which are sweep-only (the guard's park handling: currently parked → delete with the park notice; with a stale park it should not delete). For the guard, park + run finished → stale → keep. Should the guard also check children? No: guard just needs "don't delete when the lock is dead". If children are still running, the park is legitimate and the guard should keep deleting. Hmm! That's a real concern: guard+park+finished-run is the NORMAL case for a parked parent (its parked run finished by construction — the `waiting` ending is written at the end of the run). So `lockIsStale` = run finished → stale would make the guard stop guarding on every parked node. That's too blunt.

So the guard's stale rule must be: run finished AND *no live thing holds the turn*. For a dead claim (no ending), the run finishing means nothing is live. For a park, the run finishing is expected — liveness moves to the children. So the guard needs the same "children done" question... that's a GraphQL call per comment on a parked node. Hmm.

Alternative framing: the parked case's staleness is "the park should have been resumed and wasn't". The sweep decides that with the children check and then *releases the park* (writes an ending). Once released, the thread says person → the guard keeps comments. So the guard doesn't need to make the park judgement at all: it can trust the thread! The guard's stale rule then only needs to cover the dead *claim* (no ending), which is the #12 irreversible lock. And the park's escape is the sweep's release, which changes the thread — the guard, reading the thread, then keeps comments.

But the issue explicitly says guard must not delete under a stale lock, and the park case in #12 had claim+park. With claim+park: the claim is open (parked), run finished. Under the new rule "claim open + no ending + run finished → stale", a park *has* an ending (the waiting one) answering the ask. Does `shapedThread` close the claim? No — it keeps it open for `waiting`. So the guard's judgement could be: the open claim is stale unless the thread's newest event is `waiting` AND ... hmm.

Let me reconsider. Guard's rule (keep comment) when:
1. The open claim's run is stale (finished/old) AND
2. nothing live holds the ball: no park that a child could resume, i.e. the newest event is not `waiting`... 

Hmm, but then #12's second stage (claim removed, park remaining) — guard keeps deleting. That's the state #12 reached. With my change: after the sweep's park release (children done + no resume), the thread says person, so the guard keeps. The park release requires children done; in #12, was that true? The user's escape was to PATCH the waiting tag to done. That suggests they couldn't get it released. If children were all closed then, my sweep would release it. Good.

So the split is:
- **Dead claim** (claim open, no ending answering it, run finished): sweep posts the notice (releases), guard must not delete.
- **Park** (claim open, waiting ending newest, run finished, children all done, no resume): sweep posts the park-release (hands to person), guard must not delete.

For the guard's park arm to not delete under a stale park, it needs the children answer too, unless I make the guard's rule: "run finished and no live run claimed after the parked ending" → for a legitimate park (children running), the run is finished too, so the guard would stop deleting. Is that actually harmful? What does the guard protect during a park? A person commenting during a park writes something that would be read... by whom? Nobody, until the parent resumes. Then the parent's run would read it. So the guard's job during a park: prevent the comment from being read by the resumed run mid-turn? Actually the guard's real job: prevent racing a dispatch (a slash command starting a second run on a node with one in flight). During a park, no run is in flight on this node — the children are working elsewhere. A person's comment during a park is... the park's own notice says "wait for the parent". Hmm, why delete it at all? Because if the person's comment is a `/engineer`, it would start a second run on the node while the parent waits for children — the aggregation's resume would then collide. So the guard's park rule has a purpose: don't start work on a node whose turn is parked on its subtree.

Changing that to "keep during park" would weaken a real protection. So no: the guard must distinguish stale park from live park, and that requires knowing whether the children are still working — the same question as the sweep.

So: put the whole judgement in ONE domain function that both callers use, with the facts passed in. The facts:
- claim run life (finished/running/unknown) + claim age
- whether an ending answered the claim and whether the newest event is `waiting` (park)
- for a park: whether any launched child is still open (a boolean "subtree busy"), or undefined if unread.

Domain function:

```ts
export interface LockFacts {
  readonly run: RunLife;            // life of the run the claim names
  readonly ageMs: number | undefined; // claim age, fallback only
  readonly parked: boolean;          // newest turn event is a waiting ending
  /** For a park: whether a child the node is waiting on is still open; undefined = could not read. */
  readonly subtreeBusy?: boolean;
}
export function lockIsStale(facts: LockFacts): boolean {
  // A park is only stale when the subtree it waits on is finished: a park whose
  // children still work is the state parks exist for.
  if (facts.parked) {
    if (facts.subtreeBusy !== false) return false;
    return runFinishedOrTimedOut(facts);
  }
  return runFinishedOrTimedOut(facts);
}
```

Wait for park, if children are all done, then the run being "finished" is expected; the staleness is really "children done + no resume". If run life is "running"—can't be for a park (the waiting ending comes at run end... unless the run is still writing? The waiting ending is posted at the end but the run continues briefly). Let me just use the same run rule for both: run finished/old → stale, subject to subtreeBusy !== false for parks. Hmm, but if run life is "running" for a park, that's the run that just posted the waiting ending and is finishing up. `finished` needed. Fine, the same predicate.

Simplify the shape: `lockIsStale({run, ageMs, parked, subtreeBusy})`:
```
if (parked && subtreeBusy !== false) return false;
if (run === "running") return false;
if (run === "finished") return true;
return ageMs !== undefined && ageMs >= STALE_LOCK_FALLBACK_MS;
```
