 * is the same shape one step later: the turn is held open for a resume that the
 * aggregation is supposed to deliver, and if that resume is never dispatched the
 * node waits for a parent that will not come.
 *
 * ## Two questions, and the order they are asked in
 *
 * The evidence that a lock is dead is the RUN, not the clock: a claim names the run
 * that took it, and `actions/runs/{id}` says whether that run has finished. Finish +
 * no release is the whole of "nobody is coming", and it needs no timer — a run that
 * ended ten seconds ago is as dead as a run that ended ten days ago. So run facts
 * are the primary signal, and the timer is the FALLBACK for the one claim shape that
 * cannot be checked: a claim whose `run` is 0 (an older format, or an ending written
 * by something outside a run). `CLAIM_WITHOUT_RUN_MINUTES` is then the only thing
 * saying "this has been sitting too long", and it is deliberately far longer than any
 * run can live (the runner's own job bound is 240 minutes) — a false "stale" removes
 * the guard from a node a run is working on, which is the harm the guard exists to
 * prevent, so the fallback may only fire where a run could not still be alive.
 *
 * `unknown` is a third answer rather than a silent "fresh": a read that failed is not
 * an answer, and both callers say so in their own way instead of acting on a guess.
 *
 * ## Why the rule lives here
 *
 * The sweep (which releases a stale lock from outside) and the guard (which must not
 * delete a person's comment under one) are two different actions taken on ONE
 * judgement. Two spellings of the judgement would drift — the sweep releasing what
 * the guard still considers live is exactly the disagreement this module is here to
 * make impossible.
 *
 * Pure: instants arrive as arguments (`nowMs`), never from the clock.
 */

/**
 * How a lock's liveness reads.
 *
 * - `fresh` — something may still be holding it: the run is not finished, or the
 *   claim is too recent to call.
 * - `stale` — the run that took the turn has finished, and nothing released it.
 * - `unknown` — the question could not be answered (an unreadable run, an
 *   unreadable time). Not an answer, and neither caller acts on it.
 */
export type LockFreshness = "fresh" | "stale" | "unknown";

/** What GitHub says about a run, or why it could not be read. */
export type RunStatusRead =
  | { readonly read: true; readonly status: string }
  | { readonly read: false; readonly why: string };

/**
 * How long a claim that names no run id is trusted before it is called stale.
 *
 * The fallback, and only that: a claim naming a run is judged by the run. Six hours,
 * because the longest a run may live is the runner job's own 240-minute bound and a
 * queued run can sit behind it a while longer — and because a false "stale" is the
 * expensive direction. It removes the guard from a node a run is working on, which
 * is the interruption the guard exists to close.
 */
export const CLAIM_WITHOUT_RUN_MINUTES = 360;

/**
 * How long a parked turn is given for the aggregation to collect it.
 *
 * A park holds the turn open on purpose (#208): the run ended inside a child-starting
 * call, and the parent is re-invoked when the last child closes. That re-invocation
 * happens seconds after the last child closes, through its own path — so a park with
 * no open children that has survived this long has missed it. The grace is what keeps
 * this sweep out of the aggregation's way; it costs a stale park half an hour.
 */
export const PARK_COLLECTION_MINUTES = 30;

/** The claim a node's thread leaves open: which ask, which run, and when it was taken. */
export interface ClaimLock {
  /** The ask comment id the run claimed. */
  readonly ask: number;
  /** The run that took it — `0` when the claim names none, which is what the fallback is for. */
  readonly run: number;
  /** When the claim was written, as GitHub's `created_at`. Empty when the read had none. */
  readonly at: string;
